window.docContents[201].resolve({"/Colorings/#section-bipartite-graphs":{"contents":"A graph is bipartite iff its vertices can be partitioned into two parts with no\nedges within parts. In Mathlib this is defined in the obvious way via\n2-colourability, so G.IsBipartite is exactly G.Colorable 2.\n\n#source IsBipartite -- abbrev IsBipartite (G : SimpleGraph V) : Prop := G.Colorable 2\n\n\nAny complete bipartite graph is 2-colourable (bipartite). Mathlib gives the natural\ncolouring, CompleteBipartiteGraph.bicoloring, which uses Bool as the set of\ncolours (true on the right part). By Mathlib's\nnaming conventions it should be called completeBipartiteGraph.bicoloring\n(compare pathGraph.bicoloring), so we introduce that alias and use it for the rest\nof the guide.\n\nalias completeBipartiteGraph.bicoloring := CompleteBipartiteGraph.bicoloring\n\n\n#check completeBipartiteGraph.bicoloring\n  -- (V W : Type*) : (completeBipartiteGraph V W).Coloring Bool\n\n\nBy Coloring.colorable, this colouring shows that the complete bipartite graph is\nbipartite. (Mathlib also has this as IsBipartite.completeBipartiteGraph.)\n\nexample : (completeBipartiteGraph V W).IsBipartite :=\n  (completeBipartiteGraph.bicoloring V W).colorable\n\n\nWhen both parts are nonempty there is an edge, so the chromatic number is exactly\n2. In general, a graph has chromatic number 2 iff it is bipartite and has an\nedge.\n\n#check chromaticNumber_eq_two_iff -- χ G = 2 ↔ G.IsBipartite ∧ G ≠ ⊥\n\n\nFor example, colouring each vertex by its parity shows that the infinite path\npathNat has chromatic number 2: an infinite graph can have finite chromatic\nnumber.\n\nThe natural Bool-colouring of pathNat, by parity./-- The natural Bool-colouring of pathNat, by parity. -/\ndef pathNat.bicoloring : pathNat.Coloring Bool where\n  toFun n := n % 2 = 0\n  map_rel' := by rintro a b (rfl | rfl) <;> grind [top_adj]\n\n\nexample : χ pathNat = 2 :=\n  chromaticNumber_eq_two_iff.2 ⟨pathNat.bicoloring.colorable,\n    ne_bot_iff_exists_adj.2 ⟨0, 1, Or.inl rfl⟩⟩\n\n\nWe also have IsBipartiteWith which says that G is bipartite with all edges going\nbetween the disjoint sets s and t. (Note that any isolated vertex can be in\neither or neither of the parts.)\n\n#source IsBipartiteWith\n\n\nFor example, the complete bipartite graph is bipartite with parts the left and right\ncopies. (Mathlib also has this as IsBipartiteWith.completeBipartiteGraph.)\n\nexample : (completeBipartiteGraph V W).IsBipartiteWith\n    (Set.range .inl) (Set.range .inr) where\n  disjoint := by grind\n  mem_of_adj {u v} h := by grind\n\n\nMore generally, any Bool-colouring c of G makes G bipartite with parts the\nvertices coloured true and the vertices coloured false.\n\nlemma Coloring.isBipartiteWith (c : G.Coloring Bool) :\n    G.IsBipartiteWith {v | c v} {v | ¬ c v} where\n  disjoint := Set.disjoint_left.2 fun _ h h' => h' h\n  mem_of_adj _ _ h := by grind [c.valid h]\n\n\nMathlib also shows that acyclic graphs (IsAcyclic), and in particular trees\n(IsTree), are bipartite. The classical characterization, that a graph is bipartite\niff it has no odd cycles, is the subject of\nOdd Cycles and Bipartite Graphs.\n\n#check IsAcyclic.colorable_two -- (hG : G.IsAcyclic) : G.Colorable 2\n#check IsTree.colorable_two -- (hG : G.IsTree) : G.Colorable 2\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings","header":"7.4. Bipartite Graphs","id":"/Colorings/#section-bipartite-graphs"},"/Maps/#section-spanningcoe":{"contents":"Having defined induced subgraphs via SimpleGraph.comap we can also go in the other\ndirection: starting with G : SimpleGraph ↑s, where s : Set V, we can use\nSimpleGraph.map and Subtype s ↪ V to get a SimpleGraph V whose edges are\nexactly those of G. Since this graph contains all vertices of V but is otherwise\nthe same as G, it is called the spanningCoe of G.\n\nsection spanningcoe\nvariable {V : Type*}\n\n#source spanningCoe -- spanningCoe {s : Set V} (G : SimpleGraph s) :\n                    --    SimpleGraph V := G.map (Function.Embedding.subtype _)\n\n\nSince SimpleGraph.map and SimpleGraph.comap along the embedding Subtype s ↪ V\nform a Galois coinsertion, we have two very useful results relating induce and\nspanningCoe.\n\nFirstly, if we start with G : SimpleGraph ↑s (where s : Set V), take its\nspanningCoe and form the induced subgraph on s, then we recover G.\n\nexample {s : Set V} {G : SimpleGraph ↑s} :\n  G.spanningCoe.induce s = G := induce_spanningCoe\n\n\nSecondly, if we take the spanningCoe of an induced subgraph we get a graph on V\nthat is a subgraph of the original graph.\n\nexample {s : Set V} {G : SimpleGraph V} :\n  (G.induce s).spanningCoe ≤ G := spanningCoe_induce_le ..\n\n\nMoreover, recall that the support of a G : SimpleGraph V is the set of vertices\nthat are incident to at least one edge. The spanningCoe of an induced subgraph of\nG is equal to G iff the support of G is contained in the set of vertices we are\ninducing on.\n\nexample {s : Set V} {G : SimpleGraph V} :\n  (G.induce s).spanningCoe = G ↔ G.support ⊆ s := spanningCoe_induce_eq_self ..\n\nend spanningcoe\n\n\nWe have now met three ways of describing a subgraph of G : SimpleGraph V: a graph\nH ≤ G with the same vertex type V (Order), such as\n(G.induce s).spanningCoe; an induced subgraph G.induce s : SimpleGraph ↑s, whose\nvertex type is the subtype ↑s of V; and a copy Copy A G of a graph A whose\nvertices may have any type (Copies). Mathlib has a fourth,\nG.Subgraph, in which the vertex set can vary inside V. We compare all four later\nin Which Notion of Subgraph?.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps\u0009Maps","header":"5.4.1. SpanningCoe","id":"/Maps/#section-spanningcoe"}});